Overview

The Stability‑Gradient Propulsion Engine (SGPE) is a non‑thrust propulsion architecture that generates motion by creating and amplifying stability gradients. Instead of pushing mass or bending spacetime, SGPE forms a forward stability well that the craft continuously falls into. Speed is determined by gradient magnitude and entropy reinforcement.

Core Principle

Motion emerges from stability bias.

SGPE constructs two fields:

External Stability Field (Φ_ext) — derived from cosmic telemetry

Internal Stability Anchor (Φ_int) — generated by Pt/U plasma confinement

The difference between these fields forms the stability gradient:

[ \nabla \Phi = \Phi_{ext} - \Phi_{int} ]

Entropy injection deepens the forward portion of the gradient, producing drift.

System Architecture

1. Astro Field Module

Ingests cosmic telemetry (neutron‑star mergers, cosmic ray flux, spectroscopy).Outputs Φ_ext, the external stability field.

2. Fusion Anchor Module

Confinement of Pt/U plasma to generate a coherent internal stability anchor.Outputs Φ_int, the internal stability bubble.

3. Gradient Solver

Computes the stability gradient using MHD equations, Coulomb‑barrier modulation, and flux–velocity coupling.Outputs ∇Φ, the stability gradient field.

4. Entropy Engine

Converts fusion energy spikes into entropy reinforcement.Deepens the forward stability well.

5. Propulsion Drive

Locks onto the gradient and induces drift.Produces motion without thrust.

6. Sovereign Ledger

Records gradient states, entropy events, and drift vectors.Stores propulsion data in encrypted form.

